//? Title: Hopcroft minimization: actual splits and the cost of set-based refinement
//? Subtitle: Language equivalence, the original six-state DFA and measured partition scans
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
DFA minimization merges states with the same future acceptance behavior.
Hopcroft's worklist rule uses predecessor sets to split current blocks,
scheduling a smaller half when a parent is not already pending. We begin with a
three-state unary language, retain the original six-state example, and inspect
actual popped splitters, preimages and proper splits. Exact finite-product
searches verify language preservation beyond a bounded list of sample words. A
unary family then separates inverse-transition visits from whole-partition
scans: this implementation preserves the worklist rule but uses ordered sets and
scans every block, so the textbook optimized time bound is not its measured
cost. Partial, unreachable and zero-symbol machines make completion and
reachability explicit.
)

\toc

> Three states, one proper split

Consider unary words over symbol 0, accepting exactly lengths at least 2. States
0,1,2 count progress; state 2 accepts and self-loops. The initial partition is
accepting {2}, rejecting {0,1}, with both blocks pending. The implementation
pops the last pending block first: A={0,1}. Its symbol 0 preimage is X={0},
since only state 0 transitions into A. This splits {0,1} into {0} and {1}. The
parent has already been popped, so the equal-size tie schedules the inside half
{0}. The following trace and diagram show that actual split, not a
reconstruction from the final answer.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hopcroft_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/dfa_minimization_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <map>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto input = dm::algorithms::dfa_unary_threshold(2);
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::hopcroft_minimize_trace(input);
    std::print("| pop | symbol | actual splitter | actual preimage | proper splits |\n| ---: | ---: | --- | " "--- | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &pass : t.passes)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", pass.pop_index, pass.symbol,
                   pass.splitter, pass.predecessors, pass.splits.size());
    const auto &pass = t.passes.front();
    const auto &split = pass.splits.front();
    std::print("\nFirst split parent {}; inside {}; outside {}; scheduled inside {}.\n\nActual pops {}; " "proper splits {}; final reachable states {}; capture units {}.\n",
               split.parent, split.inside, split.outside,
               split.schedule == dm::algorithms::HopcroftSchedule::Inside,
               t.run.work.splitter_pops, t.run.work.splits,
               t.run.result.num_states, t.captured_entries);
    dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65, 1100);
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
    style.width = 4.3;
    style.height = 2.1;
    style.font_size = 22;
    diagram.node("parent", {0, 0},
                 std::format("Rejecting parent\n{}", split.parent), style).node(
        "in", {7, 2}, std::format("Inside preimage\n{}", split.inside), style).node("out", {7, -2}, std::format("Outside preimage\n{}", split.outside), style).edge("parent", "in", "intersect X").edge("parent", "out", "subtract X").text({3.5, 4.5}, std::format("Actual popped A = {}; preimage X = {}", pass.splitter, pass.predecessors), 24).save_svg("build/hopcroft_tiny.svg");
    if (t.run.result.num_states != 3 || t.run.work.splits != 1 ||
        !dm::algorithms::dfa_equivalence(input, t.run.result).equivalent)
        throw std::runtime_error("tiny minimization");
}
