> Derive state equivalence and stable refinement

For a complete DFA with transition function delta and accepting set F, define p
equivalent to q when every finite suffix w gives the same acceptance from both
states:

$$p\sim q\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\forall w\in\Sigma^*,\ [\delta^*(p,w)\in F]=[\delta^*(q,w)\in F].$$
\alttext(Two states are equivalent exactly when every suffix word is either accepted by both or rejected by both.)

The empty suffix forces accepting and rejecting states into separate initial
blocks. For a current splitter A and symbol c, form its preimage

$$X_c(A)=\{s: \delta(s,c)\in A\}.$$
\alttext(The preimage contains every state whose c-transition enters the popped splitter.)

Whenever a block Y meets both X and its complement, replace Y by its
intersection and difference. Equivalent states never separate: if one
c-successor enters a union of equivalence classes, the other's equivalent
successor must do so too. At a stable partition, members of a block share
acceptance and send each symbol into the same block. Induction on suffix length
then proves that every member accepts exactly the same suffixes. Thus the stable
quotient merges precisely the equivalent states. Only its reachable blocks
belong in the minimal machine for the given start.

A pending parent is replaced by both halves, preserving its outstanding
distinction. For a nonpending parent, processing one half suffices once the
parent distinction is covered: entry into the other child is entry into the
parent minus entry into the chosen child. The currently popped splitter remains
an immutable copy across all symbols, even if its original block is refined
during processing. Choosing the smaller half limits repeated newly scheduled
memberships. The implementation preserves intersection-first partition order,
last-in-first-out splitters and ascending symbols. An equal-size nonpending
split chooses the intersection half. These choices affect history and numbering,
not the accepted language.

> Preserve the original six-state example

The original two-symbol transition table has rows (1,2), (0,3), (4,5), (4,5),
(4,5), (5,5), with start 0 and accepting states 2,3,4. All six states are
reachable. States 0,1 have the same future language; so do states 2,3,4; state 5
is the rejecting sink. The three classes are distinguishable: acceptance
separates the middle class immediately, and the one-symbol suffix 1 separates
{0,1} from {5}.

The next program prints actual internal partition IDs and the returned
quotient's transition table separately. Internal IDs index completed-state
blocks; returned states use ascending-symbol BFS from the start and can have
different numbers. The diagram shows the actual internal classes. Language
equality uses exhaustive exploration of the finite product automaton: it proves
equality for all finite words, rather than only the original length-at-most 6
check. Moore's independently implemented signature refinement supplies an
additional state-count comparison.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hopcroft_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/dfa_minimization_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <map>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto input = dm::algorithms::hopcroft_example_dfa();
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::hopcroft_minimize_trace(input);
    const auto eq = dm::algorithms::dfa_equivalence(input, t.run.result);
    const auto moore = dm::algorithms::moore_minimize(input);
    std::print("Original states {}; reachable minimized states {}; Moore states {}; exact equivalence {}; " "product pairs examined {}.\n",
               input.num_states, t.run.result.num_states, moore.num_states,
               eq.equivalent, eq.pairs_examined);
    std::print("\n| original state | final internal block | accepting |\n| ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    std::map<int, std::vector<int>> groups;
    for (int i = 0; i < input.num_states; ++i) {
        groups[t.run.blocks[i]].push_back(i);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", i, t.run.blocks[i],
                   bool(input.accept[i]));
    }
    std::print("\n| returned BFS state | symbol0 destination | symbol1 destination | accepting |\n| ---: | " "---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < t.run.result.num_states; ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, t.run.result.delta[i][0],
                   t.run.result.delta[i][1], bool(t.run.result.accept[i]));
    std::print("\n| actual pop | symbol | splitter | preimage | proper splits |\n| ---: | ---: | --- | --- " "| ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &pass : t.passes)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", pass.pop_index, pass.symbol,
                   pass.splitter, pass.predecessors, pass.splits.size());
    dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65, 1100);
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
    style.width = 4.8;
    style.height = 2.3;
    style.font_size = 22;
    std::size_t column = 0;
    for (const auto & [id, members] : groups) {
        diagram.node(std::to_string(id), {7. * column, 0},
                     std::format("Internal block {}\n{}", id, members), style);
        ++column;
    }
    diagram.text({7, 3}, "Actual final partition of the original six states", 25).save_svg("build/hopcroft_original.svg");
    if (!eq.equivalent || t.run.result.num_states != 3 || moore.num_states != 3)
        throw std::runtime_error("original equivalence");
}
