> Validation and primary reference

Three sanitizer tests cover all 972 two-state partial binary-alphabet DFAs,
reconstruct every actual preimage/split/partition and capture count, and verify
selected work. Exact product searches prove language preservation and
distinguish every pair of returned states. Fixture, threshold, malformed-input,
policy, empty/zero-alphabet and ownership controls also execute. Frozen
pre-conversion comparisons preserve all quotient fields across 4096 additional
machines, including nonboolean acceptance bytes. An independent Python
table-filling oracle checks all state-pair equivalences for 6804
complete/partial machines and independently explores their language products.
Existing automata/Brzozowski tests, compiled documentation examples, symbol
hovers, actual MEP programs, inspected figures and verified Markdown/HTML
result/image exports are conversion gates.

The original method is [John Hopcroft, An n log n Algorithm for Minimizing
States in a Finite
Automaton](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~cdm/resources/Hopcroft1971.pdf), Stanford
report STAN-CS-71-190, 1971; published in Theory of Machines and Computations,
pp 189–196, [DOI
10.1016/B978-0-12-417750-5.50022-1](https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-0-12-417750-5.50022-1).
Its implementation discussion motivates the distinction between the worklist
idea and efficient affected-block updates. The small fixtures here and the unary
family are explicit native inputs, not digitized experimental timings from that
paper.
